================================================================================
SPEC 002 - AGENTIC SENTIMENT ANALYSIS FOR X DATA
================================================================================

File: specs/002-agentic-sentiment-analysis/specify.plan

TITLE
-----
Agentic, Multi-Step Sentiment Analysis Pipeline for X (Twitter) Data

PURPOSE
-------
This specification governs the design and implementation of an agentic,
multi-step natural language processing subsystem for sentiment analysis
of X (Twitter) data.

The system is designed to operate downstream of the core data ingestion
and cleaning pipeline defined in Spec 001. It consumes cleaned tweet text
artifacts and produces structured, auditable sentiment outputs.

This subsystem prioritizes modularity, explainability, repeatability,
and cost-aware LLM usage.

SCOPE
-----
IN SCOPE:
- Agentic sentiment analysis using LangChain
- Multi-agent workflows (committee-style analysis)
- Use of OpenRouter-compatible LLMs
- Structured JSON outputs for sentiment and emotion
- CLI-invokable execution, per year or per dataset
- Optional integration into the full pipeline run

OUT OF SCOPE:
- Raw data ingestion
- CSV cleaning or normalization
- Schema changes to upstream data artifacts
- Model fine-tuning or training
- Real-time inference or API services
- UI or dashboarding

DEPENDENCIES
------------
- Requires outputs from Spec 001:
  - Cleaned tweet text
  - Tweet identifiers
  - Year-based data partitioning

- Must not refactor or alter upstream pipeline logic unless explicitly
  required for integration hooks.

ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLES
------------------------
1. Agentic Design
   - Each agent has a single, well-defined responsibility.
   - Agents operate independently and communicate only via structured data.

2. Deterministic Interfaces
   - All agents must emit strict, machine-parseable JSON.
   - No free-form text outputs are permitted beyond rationale fields.

3. Parallelism
   - Independent agents should execute in parallel where possible.

4. Supervisory Arbitration
   - A supervisor agent resolves disagreements and calibrates confidence.
   - The supervisor may downgrade confidence or mark ambiguity.

5. Cost Awareness
   - Cheaper models may be used for simpler agents.
   - Stronger models reserved for arbitration and edge cases.

6. Repeatability
   - Identical inputs must yield structurally consistent outputs.
   - Outputs must be comparable across years.

AGENT ROLES
-----------
Minimum required agents:

1. Normalizer Agent
   - Expands slang and emojis
   - Removes URLs and usernames
   - Preserves sentiment-bearing tokens

2. Polarity Agent
   - Classifies sentiment as positive, neutral, or negative

3. Emotion Agent
   - Classifies emotion using a fixed taxonomy
     (e.g., joy, anger, disappointment, excitement, neutral)

4. Sarcasm Detection Agent
   - Flags potential sarcasm or irony

5. Supervisor Agent
   - Reviews all agent outputs
   - Produces final sentiment decision
   - Assigns calibrated confidence
   - Flags ambiguity or low confidence cases

OPTIONAL AGENTS (DEFERRED)
--------------------------
- Topic Inference Agent (refs, teams, commercials, halftime, etc.)
- Toxicity / Abuse Detection Agent
- Language Detection Agent

OUTPUT CONTRACT
---------------
Each processed tweet must yield a single structured record:

{
  "tweet_id": "<string>",
  "year": <int>,
  "final": {
    "sentiment": "positive | neutral | negative",
    "confidence": <float 0.0-1.0>
  },
  "agents": {
    "polarity": {...},
    "emotion": {...},
    "sarcasm": {...},
    "normalizer": {...}
  },
  "flags": [ "<string>" ]
}

INTEGRATION REQUIREMENTS
------------------------
- The subsystem must be invokable independently via CLI.
- The subsystem must be optionally callable from the full pipeline.
- Year-based execution must be supported.
- Output artifacts must be written to a deterministic location.
- Output path standard:
  - `outputs/analytics/<year>/sentiment_agentic.jsonl` (per-tweet records)
  - `outputs/analytics/<year>/sentiment_agentic_summary.json` (aggregate counts)

CONSTITUTION ALIGNMENT
----------------------
- Year-scoped paths are required; no cross-year overwrite.
- No writes to `data/raw/` are permitted.
- Existing stage contracts remain intact; integration is additive.
- Baseline analytics modules remain unchanged and continue to run.
- README updates are mandatory with agent roles, model choices, cost controls,
  CLI usage, and known limitations.

DOCUMENTATION REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------
- README must be created or updated to document:
  - Agent roles
  - Model choices
  - Execution instructions
  - Known limitations
  - Cost considerations

END OF SPECIFY.PLAN
================================================================================
